{
  "description": "The `when` cell of the on-stack half of the handler-boundary rule; see `an-every-handler-stop-is-not-consumed-by-its-registering-call` for the rationale. The `\"start\"` handler fires synchronously during registration, so `setup` IS still on the stack without needing an enclosing `wait`, and the `stop` must still raise `ol-stop-outside-proc` (spec/error-model.md:119) rather than quietly ending `setup`. Its `return` counterpart is the pre-existing `interaction-events/when/when-return-in-handler-inside-proc`. Added in review round 3 after the QA reviewer measured that removing ONLY the handler boundary's `stop` conversion — leaving its `return` conversion intact, at all four sites — survives the whole corpus; round 4 then found the same blindness per handler KIND, which the siblings close.",
